A||||13||Spektyr||New Fiero Owner||||6||
Z||000000||Spektyr||04-19-2001||10:20 PM||||Alright!  Just got my first Fiero!<P>White 1985 (I think, waiting for title) GT, V6, 4 speed.  Put seat covers on it (because they've pretty much all got ripped seats) and matching floor mats.<P>Body is pretty good, the driver's side ground effects have a crack in them, paint is rough in a few spots including the hood.  Needs a bath pretty badly, but I'm not worrying about that too much at the moment.<P>Engine seems pretty ok, it's all stock, but it's missing the oil cap (??!!).  Stuck a rag in it for the time being to prevent crud from getting in there.  None of the local parts shops seem to have them in stock.<P>The front suspension bushings are starting to crack, haven't had a look at the back end yet.<P>So why am I posting?  First off, I'm just stoked to have a fun little sports car.<BR>Secondly I'm looking for some advice on what to start working on.<P>Things I know I want to improve/fix.<P>Suspension (goal is improved performance without shaking my fillings out).<BR>Body (not top priority)<BR>Motor (ensure smooth running, possibly rebuild for turbo, or do a V8 swap)<P>My budget will probably be around 500 bucks a month tops, at least till the end of the year.<P>So what's the first things I should do to make this a fun ride?||||reg||6
Z||000001||HummelHund||04-19-2001||10:25 PM||||It's a notchback, right? To tell if it's an '85 or not, look for a third brake light. If it has one, it's an early '86. If not, it's an '85. I think... Someone correct me if I'm wrong.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spektyr||04-19-2001||10:31 PM||||Notchback means it has the two vents over the engine, one on each side, right?  This one does.  No 3rd brake light, headrest speakers.  Guess that means it's an '85.<P>Also, the guy that sold it to me said the motor for the headlights wasn't shutting off, so his dad just cut the motor wires (retard).  They're up at least, but I'm still going to have to fix that.<P>Um... how to I open the hood?  There's a button on the dash that shows an open trunk, but nothing seems to happen if I push it up or down.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sjp777||04-19-2001||10:33 PM||||It's an 85. Speakers give it away.<P>and they all have the vents.<P>hood release is on the driver's side dash, underneath the vent nearest the door.<P>gas door release is on the driver's side rear pillar behind the door glass<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||04-19-2001||10:38 PM||||One other question to add...<P>What's the ballpark figure to expect if I get the motor rebuilt to handle a turbo, and buy a decent aftermarket turbo.<P>Looking for at least 250hp from the setup, want to know how much cash I'll need to save before I do this (if I decide turbo V6 is the way I want to go)||||reg||1
Z||000005||turbo86se||04-19-2001||11:57 PM||||I wouldn't put a turbo on a motor with a lot of miles, especially if you just bought it. Now if you were the original owner and took good care of it, that would be different.<P>Figure about $3000 for the new turbo kit, if you're not mechanically inclined like me, another $2000 to install it. Could be less, mine was done at IRM.<P>If you ever come to Myrtle Beach, LMK, I'll take you for a test-drive in a turbo Fiero, she's sleeping in the garage!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektyr||04-20-2001||12:33 AM||||Yup, this car has 107k on the odometer.  However, I'm not foolish enough to just slap a turbo on it.  If I decide I need a hairdryer I'll have the engine rebuilt to handle it.<P>Or maybe just buy one fully built with the turbo installed if that's cheaper.<P>Ideas anyone?<P>Also, where am I going to find the oil cap for this thing?  The rag in the valve cover doesn't exactly say "High Performance" ya know...||||reg||1
Z||000007||GarethWright||04-20-2001||02:20 AM||||Welcome Spektyr,<BR>Oil Cap: <A HREF="http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/prodinfo_pegasus.asp?number=64789&variation=&aitem=4&mitem=4" TARGET=_blank>http://www.secureleadercom.com/store/prodinfo_pegasus.asp?number=64789&variation=&aitem=4&mitem=4</A> <P>Also check out <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com</A> <BR>get a free catalog, I use it for a price guide, and they sometimes have items Autozone doesn't  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Happy Motoring,<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000008||Spektyr||04-20-2001||03:37 AM||||Thanks, ordered the cap and the catalog.<P>Just got done reading all the neato suspension stuff.<P>Earlier today on a WOT highway on ramp run I had a disturbing handling experience.  Right as I hit the last part of the curve onto the highway going about 70mph the front end kind of 'skittered' a bit and acted like it wanted to slide sideways.  I'm guessing I need a new steering dampner (at the very least).  The front end also tends to jolt rather annoying over moderate bumps (yeah, it's a sports car, I know) and it jerks the wheel just a bit.<P>My plan is to clothe the suspension with all poly, put in strong anti-sways front and rear, upgrade shocks, and possibly springs as well.<P>Judging by the way the car acts, what parts need to get replaced first?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||04-20-2001||07:46 AM||||Welcome to the wonderful world of Fieros.<P>I have an 85SE v-6 4spd and an 85GT auto and love both.<P>I put a rear sway bar on the SE and was very pleased with the results.  I also bought and installed a Grooms long block in the SE through Ed Parks of the Fiero Factory ((256)420-5391) fierofactory@juno.com.<BR>  (http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#top)  <P>There will be a Fiero Swap Meet at his place on the weekend of April 27 near Huntsville, AL.<P>It sounds like you are in need of control arm bushings, tie-rod ends, and maybe ball joints.  Mine is getting sloppy too.<P>These cars are very sensitive to rear alignment.  When I replaced the rear struts I simply slapped them on and drove to the tire store for new tires and an alignment.  To say the least the ride was exciting.  The back end kept trying to take the lead.  It was rock steady with new treads and correct alignment.<P>Porting the intake and exhaust manifolds will give you cheap HP (maybe around 6 or 8, next best thing to headers) and isn't hard to do at all.<P>Here is an excelent link to a wealth of Fiero information.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html</A> <P>Also try the Search Function on this forum and the link to Ogre's Cave at the bottom of the page.  A lot of us have been around for a while and have discussed just about everything.<P>Most importantly just enjoy your new ride and the friendships you'll develope here.<P>Hope to meet you some day and see your car.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||7
Z||000010||Steve Normington||04-20-2001||11:27 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spektyr:<BR>Notchback means it has the two vents over the engine, one on each side, right?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Notchback means that the sail pillars (the ones behind the doors) slope to the deck just behind the engine vent. The other type is the fastback which means that the sail pillars slope all the way to the rear of the deck. Check out this thread.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009777.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009777.html</A> <P>The signature images on sqoach's and Phil's posts are notchbacks and the image on Jaei Sun's post is a fastback.<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6<p>[This message has been edited by Steve Normington (edited 04-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Spektyr||04-20-2001||11:45 AM||||Okay, it's definitely a notchback.  I'm going to pop out the mesh grill under the engine vents to help the cooling (read that somewhere here... this site rocks) and put in a 160 thermostat and 175 fan switch.  Dropping under hood temp even just a few degrees will probably give me a few extra ponies (denser air) and make me feel a bit better about the temp this car runs at.  (currently in the 200 range, normal but a bit higher than I'd like).<P>As for the handling, most of my problems seem to be in the front end.  It has a very mild push in the rear when I really stop on the gas, the back pushes slightly to the right when the tires spin, but it's very slight and very predictable.  About 1/8th right turn of the steering wheel corrects it.<P>The steering is very tight (if a little heavy) with no 'dead spot'.  (If I wiggle the wheel, she wiggles her butt).  My main concern is that when I go over a bump she acts a little jumpy, and at highway speeds if I wiggle the wheel, the body roll is scary.  It feels pretty easy to toss the rear end if I try to change lanes fast.  Poly bushings will probably clear up the steering issue (right?), and anti-sways should vastly improve the confidence of the car.  I want the best handling I can get without spending several thousand dollars, and without creating a bone-jarring ride.<P>What would everyone suggest?<P>(btw, if I drop ride height too much, I can't garage the car, my driveway is quite steep)||||reg||1
Z||000012||sqoach||04-20-2001||12:20 PM||||Notchbacks rule!<P>Hehe...so we've confirmed that it's a notchback, but not if it's an SE or a GT. (unless I missed that)<P>And not to sound like an a$$ or anything, but $500 a month makes it pretty hard to upgrade. Unless you use all your money for your car. Unexpected things happen, and it breaks the piggy bank.<P>Anyway, just have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||14
Z||000013||Spektyr||04-20-2001||02:06 PM||||That's $500 a month FOR upgrades.  After I pay rent, the Lincoln's car payment, insurance, food, gas, and an allowance for various other things that always eat up your pocket money.<P>Sure that makes coming up with 8-9k to have Archie install a new V8 a rather arduous task, but for the basic stuff it'll do just fine.<P>Ya see, if I get the suspension, brakes, and a minor bit of engine work done, the Fiero will make a good getaway car when I rob a bank to pay for the rest of the mods.<P>Um... on second thought, ignore that last bit.  hehe<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)||||reg||1
